### Section D: Dark/Light Mode Toggle

**In HTML** — Add `data-theme="light"` to `<html>`. Add a toggle button inside the navbar, after the nav-links:

```html
<button class="theme-toggle" id="themeToggle" aria-label="Toggle dark mode">
  <svg class="icon-sun" ...><!-- sun SVG --></svg>
  <svg class="icon-moon" ...><!-- moon SVG --></svg>
</button>
```

**In CSS** — All color custom properties under `:root` define the light mode. Add a `[data-theme="dark"]` block that overrides every `--color-*` token. This means zero conditional styling elsewhere in the stylesheet — any component that uses a CSS variable gets dark mode automatically.

```css
[data-theme="dark"] {
  --color-bg: #08111e;
  --color-bg-alt: #0d1a2b;
  /* ... all dark tokens from design system above ... */
}
```

Navbar dark mode: the `rgba(255,255,255,0.94)` background must become `rgba(8,17,30,0.94)` — this is achieved via a `--color-nav-bg` token.

**In JS:**

```js
const themeToggle = document.getElementById('themeToggle');
const root = document.documentElement;

// Respect system preference as default, override with stored value
const stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
const prefersDark = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
const initial = stored || (prefersDark ? 'dark' : 'light');
root.setAttribute('data-theme', initial);

themeToggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const next = root.getAttribute('data-theme') === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
  root.setAttribute('data-theme', next);
  localStorage.setItem('theme', next);
});
```

The `data-theme` attribute must be set by an inline script in `<head>` (before CSS loads) to prevent a flash of wrong theme. Add this as the first `<script>` tag, inline, in `<head>`:

```html
<script>
  (function(){
    var t = localStorage.getItem('theme') ||
      (window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light');
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', t);
  })();
</script>
```

### Section E: Hero Redesign

**Aurora/Geometric Background (CSS-only):**

The hero background uses layered pseudo-elements with radial gradients and a CSS `@keyframes` animation. No canvas or external library needed. Structure:

```
.hero { position: relative; overflow: hidden; }
.hero::before { /* large slow-moving radial aurora blob */ }
.hero::after  { /* second smaller blob, different color */ }
.hero-bg-geo  { /* retained element, now carries CSS-animated mesh lines */ }
```

The aurora effect is achieved with `filter: blur(80px)` on large colored ellipses that slowly scale and shift using `animation: aurora-drift 14s ease-in-out infinite alternate`. This is a proven, GPU-friendly technique used by Stripe, Linear, and Vercel for hero sections.

